WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:07.860
Welcome to the OCP S.A.F.E. or S.A.F.E.
program.

2
00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.860
In this course, you'll gain a foundational
understanding

3
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.860
of how the S.A.F.E. framework improves
device security,

4
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.860
streamlines audits,

5
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.860
and supports supply chain assurance across
modern data centers.

6
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.860
Today's data centers use a diverse set of
devices,

7
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:30.860
CPUs, GPUs, FPGAs, NICs, and storage
systems,

8
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:35.860
all of which run firmware or microcode that
may be updated over time.

9
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.860
These components form part of a complex
software supply chain.

10
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:43.860
Assuring the security and integrity of
those devices is essential,

11
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.860
especially as regulatory expectations
increase globally.

12
00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.860
Historically, each customer or hyperscaler

13
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.860
would request their own audit of a device,

14
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:58.860
creating a patchwork of duplicated work and
inconsistent reviews.

15
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.860
This model led to delays, inefficiencies,

16
00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.860
and late discovery of vulnerabilities often
during production or deployment.

17
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.860
OCP S.A.F.E. was developed to address this
problem.

18
00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.860
It allows a single structured review to
serve multiple customers.

19
00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.860
The goal is simple, reduce audit overhead,

20
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.860
share trustworthy results,

21
00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.860
and enable faster time to market

22
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.860
while strengthening the overall security
posture of device firmware.

23
00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:33.860
S.A.F.E. assessments are conducted by
approved security review providers or SRPs.

24
00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:39.860
These are trusted third-party firms
selected for their independence and
technical depth.

25
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.860
Each engagement starts with a scope
definition.

26
00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.860
The device vendor and the SRP align on
which scopes will be in scope

27
00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:53.860
and within each scope which sections apply
to the device under test.

28
00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.860
As part of this early phase, the device
vendor is expected

29
00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:01.860
to socialize the scope definition with
their cloud service provider.

30
00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.860
Scope 1 addresses privileged firmware and
software on a single device.

31
00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:12.860
It includes design review, threat modeling,
code analysis, and code review.

32
00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:19.180
Scope 2 examines architectural isolation of
critical components,

33
00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:23.180
like management co-processors or TEEs.

34
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:28.180
It relies on hardware design and code
reviews, not live attacks.

35
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:32.180
Scope 3 focuses on resilience to physical
attacks

36
00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.180
and the protection of long-term secrets.

37
00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:41.180
It includes an analysis of SoC design and
fault injection protections.

38
00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:44.180
S.A.F.E. reviews are document-driven.

39
00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:49.180
The SRP examines firmware, HDL, design
documentation,

40
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:54.180
and threat models using white box methods
rather than reverse engineering.

41
00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.180
Device vendors submit artifacts including
source code,

42
00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.180
firmware binaries, and any relevant
security models.

43
00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.180
The SRP reviews and identifies any
weaknesses.

44
00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.180
The review is collaborative.

45
00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:14.180
Vendors address findings and resubmit until
the assessment meets the safe criteria.

46
00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.180
The SRP produces two deliverables.

47
00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.180
The first is a detailed report for the
vendor's internal use.

48
00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:27.180
The second is a concise, signed summary
known as the short-form report.

49
00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:32.180
The device vendor can choose to embargo a
short-form report

50
00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:36.180
where there is concern about publicly
disclosing certain findings.

51
00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:41.180
This short-form report includes the
firmware hash, review scope,

52
00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:45.180
SRP name, and whether any vulnerabilities
were found.

53
00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.180
Short-form reports are submitted to OCP's
GitHub repository

54
00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.180
and optionally listed in the OCP
marketplace.

55
00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:59.180
Consumers can verify audit status without
repeating the review.

56
00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:03.180
S.A.F.E. ensures that firmware security
reviews happen early

57
00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:07.180
and can be reused across vendors and cloud
platforms.

58
00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:12.180
This supports consistent assurance and
scalable compliance.

59
00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.180
The short-form supports reference integrity
manifests or RIMs,

60
00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:21.180
which help match measured device values to
the firmware that was reviewed.

61
00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:25.180
S.A.F.E. does not replace regulatory
programs like FIPS.

62
00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:31.180
It complements them by focusing
specifically on firmware level review
outcomes

63
00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.180
and transparency.

64
00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:38.180
Several companies have already
participated, AMD submitted a GPU,

65
00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:44.180
SK hynix reviewed an SSD, and Intel
demonstrated end-to-end attestation.

66
00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:49.180
As the program evolves, more SRPs and
device types will be supported.

67
00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:52.180
S.A.F.E. is designed to be flexible,

68
00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:57.180
accommodating a range of architectures, use
cases, and threat models.

69
00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.180
In this course will guide you through how
to prepare for a safe review,

70
00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:07.180
define scope, engage with an SRP, and
submit results.

71
00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:12.180
S.A.F.E. reflects a shift towards shared
security responsibility.

72
00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:16.180
It builds trust between producers and
consumers,

73
00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:20.180
and reduces time to assurance across the
ecosystem.
